@meta
id: 29-05
title: Wrapping text in a pair you choose
chapter: 29
type: lesson
skills: surround, packadd
par: 48
minutes: 5
vscode: select the word, then hope the editor's auto-surround is on
open: webhooks.md
vimrc: set packpath^=. | packloadall!

@brief
The dojo ships a surround plugin with this lesson. It is sitting in
`pack/dojo/start/surround/plugin/surround.vim` in this very directory, it
is seventy lines of vimscript plus its comments, and it is real: you can read it, and the
edit you make with it is graded like any other edit. You did not install
it. Nothing was downloaded. That is the point of the chapter — a plugin is
a file.

What it gives you is one new verb: *surround*. Vim has verbs for deleting
and changing text; it has none for putting a pair of characters around
text, which is a thing developers do forty times a day. The plugin adds
one.

In webhooks.md, two identifiers are sitting in the prose as plain words.
Both should be in backticks, the way every other code reference in this
doc already is. Change nothing else.

@keys
| `ys{motion}{char}` | surround whatever the motion covers with `{char}` |
| `ysiw"` | put double quotes around the word under the cursor |
| `ysiw)` | put brackets around it — `(` and `)` both work |

@hints
The verb is `ys`, and like every vim verb it wants a motion. Which text
object describes "this word"?
--
`ysiw` followed by the character you want. The backtick is a character
like any other.
--
`/webhookSecret` `Enter`, then ``ysiw` ``, then search for the second one
and do it again.

@solution
    /webhookSecret<Enter>  ysiw`  /retryLimit<Enter>  ysiw`  :wq<Enter>

Read `ys` as "you surround". It takes a motion exactly the way `d` and `c`
do, so everything you already know about motions transfers on day one:
`ysiw` for a word, `ysi(` for what is inside brackets, `yst,` for
everything up to the next comma, `ys$` to the end of the line.

Open `pack/dojo/start/surround/plugin/surround.vim` and look at the last
four lines. They are four mappings. The whole plugin is a couple of
functions and four mappings, and vim sourced it at startup because of
where the file sits — under a `pack/*/start/*/plugin/` directory on the
`packpath`. That is vim's built-in package system, and it has needed no
help from a plugin manager since vim 8.

The real one is tpope's vim-surround. It is bigger than this because it
handles tags, function calls, indentation and repeating with `.`, but the
verbs are the ones you have just used.

@start pack/dojo/start/surround/plugin/surround.vim
" dojo-surround.vim - a small, honest surround plugin.
" Shipped inside this lesson. You did not install it; the dojo did.
"
"   ds{char}           delete the surrounding pair
"   cs{old}{new}       change one surrounding pair into another
"   ys{motion}{char}   surround the text the motion covers
"   {Visual}S{char}    surround the selection
"
" Brackets come in pairs: ( [ { < all give you the matching closer.
" Anything else is used on both sides, so " gives "..." and ` gives `...`.

if exists('g:loaded_dojo_surround')
  finish
endif
let g:loaded_dojo_surround = 1

let s:pairs = {
      \ '(': '()', ')': '()',
      \ '[': '[]', ']': '[]',
      \ '{': '{}', '}': '{}',
      \ '<': '<>', '>': '<>',
      \ }

function! s:delims(char) abort
  return get(s:pairs, a:char, a:char . a:char)
endfunction

" The text between the pair, without the pair itself.
function! s:inner(char) abort
  let save = @z
  let @z = ''
  silent execute 'normal! "zyi' . a:char
  let text = @z
  let @z = save
  return text
endfunction

" A visual selection covering the pair AND what is between it. vi" selects
" the inside; o h o l then walks each end out by one character onto the
" delimiters. Doing it this way rather than with va" matters: va" also
" swallows the whitespace next to the string.
function! s:select(char) abort
  return 'vi' . a:char . 'ohol'
endfunction

function! s:replace(char, text) abort
  let save = @z
  let @z = a:text
  silent execute 'normal! ' . s:select(a:char) . '"zp'
  let @z = save
endfunction

function! s:ds(char) abort
  let inner = s:inner(a:char)
  if inner ==# ''
    return
  endif
  call s:replace(a:char, inner)
endfunction

function! s:cs(old, new) abort
  let inner = s:inner(a:old)
  if inner ==# ''
    return
  endif
  let d = s:delims(a:new)
  call s:replace(a:old, d[0] . inner . d[1])
endfunction

function! s:ys(type) abort
  let d = s:delims(nr2char(getchar()))
  let save = @z
  if a:type ==# 'line'
    silent execute "normal! '[V']\"zy"
    call setreg('z', d[0] . "\n" . @z . d[1], 'V')
    silent execute "normal! '[V']\"zp"
  else
    silent execute "normal! `[v`]\"zy"
    let @z = d[0] . @z . d[1]
    silent execute "normal! `[v`]\"zp"
  endif
  let @z = save
endfunction

function! s:vs(char) abort
  let d = s:delims(a:char)
  let save = @z
  silent execute 'normal! gv"zy'
  let @z = d[0] . @z . d[1]
  silent execute 'normal! gv"zp'
  let @z = save
endfunction

nnoremap <silent> ds :<C-u>call <SID>ds(nr2char(getchar()))<CR>
nnoremap <silent> cs :<C-u>call <SID>cs(nr2char(getchar()), nr2char(getchar()))<CR>
nnoremap <silent> ys :<C-u>set operatorfunc=<SID>ys<CR>g@
xnoremap <silent> S :<C-u>call <SID>vs(nr2char(getchar()))<CR>

@start webhooks.md
# Webhooks

Every webhook we send is signed. The signature is in the
`X-Order-Signature` header and is an HMAC of the raw body.

The payload is signed with the webhookSecret from the environment. Verify
it before you trust the body, and compare with a constant-time function,
not `===`.

Delivery is retried with backoff. Retries stop after retryLimit attempts
and the event is written to `webhook_dead_letter`.

@expected webhooks.md
# Webhooks

Every webhook we send is signed. The signature is in the
`X-Order-Signature` header and is an HMAC of the raw body.

The payload is signed with the `webhookSecret` from the environment. Verify
it before you trust the body, and compare with a constant-time function,
not `===`.

Delivery is retried with backoff. Retries stop after `retryLimit` attempts
and the event is written to `webhook_dead_letter`.
